  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 132 PM MDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nAs expected, it's hot across the I-25 corridor and plains this\nafternoon. Temperatures have already eclipsed 100F at several\nlocations, with places like KDEN not too far behind (currently\n99F). These temperatures are rather impressive, especially given\nthe lingering haze/smoke across the plains along with some\nscattered mid-level cloud cover. We probably have another couple\ndegrees of warming this afternoon, with highs generally hitting\nthe 100-103F range across the I-25 corridor.\n\nWhat remains to be seen is how many showers are able to develop\nthis afternoon. ACARS soundings and SPC Mesoanalysis show very\nmeager instability, but DCAPE currently sits at about 1800 J/kg.\nIt also seems possible (probable) that the HRRR is overdoing the\namount of convection this afternoon, just based on satellite\ntrends to start the afternoon. We still expected isolated to\nwidely scattered showers, which would likely produce more wind\n(gusts up to 50 mph) than rain.\n\nMeanwhile, a strong cold front is still on track to arrive later\nthis afternoon. The front looks to be moving through Cheyenne at\nthe moment, with notable pressure rises across eastern Wyoming.\nThis is expected to reach the Denver metro by about 5-6PM today,\nwhich should provide substantial relief from the heat... while\nalso advecting some near surface smoke into the area for the\nevening hours. While smoke will persist through most of Tuesday,\nwe will at least see much cooler temperatures for the plains...\nwith highs in the mid/upper 70s. Temperatures will warm back into\nthe low to mid 80s by Wednesday.\n\nThe higher elevations won't feel the effects of the cold front,\nand will instead have to contend with fire weather concerns over\nthe next few days, as zonal flow aloft begins to strengthen. A Red\nFlag Warning has been issued for North Park and Middle Park on\nTuesday and additional highlights may be needed for Wednesday and\nThursday.\n\nOur brief cooldown will quickly be replaced by hotter and drier\nconditions as the upper ridge once again strengthens over the \nFour Corners region late this week into the upcoming weekend. With\nmid-level temperatures once again approaching 20-22C this would \nlikely lead to highs approaching 100F on Saturday and Sunday. The \nposition of the upper ridge would also limit any chance of monsoon\nmoisture making a return until at least early next week.",
